---
/**
 * SHELL-009 — /docs/theming: the content-shaped cold-boot surface FLOW-SHELL-02
 * lands on. Authored prose states only what the shell does today, pinned to the
 * four copy-stable anchors ('ship no tokens', 'oklch', 'prefers-color-scheme',
 * 'toggle' — SHELL-010 may reword around them, never remove them). The
 * TokenTable live demo and Expressive Code theming are sprint-05's remainder on
 * this same route and are deliberately absent, not faked.
 */
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Theming — RNR AI Elements">
  <main class="theming">
    <h1>Theming</h1>

    <p>
      The registry items ship no tokens of their own. Each one you copy in
      reads its colors from your app's Tailwind v4 <code>@theme</code> block.
      Re-theming your product never touches registry code. There is no
      <code>tailwind.config.js</code> and no theme setting to configure.
    </p>

    <p>
      This site reads its colors from one token sheet of plain CSS custom
      properties — no Tailwind, no <code>@theme</code> block. Every color in
      that sheet is an <code>oklch</code> value, and the dark values are the
      defaults. The sheet itself never reads your OS
      <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> setting. A script in the page head
      resolves your theme before first paint: your stored choice first, then
      your OS scheme, then dark.
    </p>

    <p>
      The toggle in the site header is the live control. It flips a
      <code>light</code> class on the root element, and every token re-resolves
      in place — no reload, no second stylesheet.
    </p>

    <section id="textclasscontext">
      <h2>The TextClassContext seam</h2>
      <p>
        React Native has no style cascade: a parent view cannot restyle the
        text inside a child component just by wrapping it. Registry parents
        that need to restyle nested text — message, prompt-input, tool, and
        the other list-bearing components — instead publish their class
        strings through the <code>TextClassContext</code> provider from the
        text module. Those class strings are ordinary Tailwind classes, so
        they resolve against your <code>@theme</code> block exactly like the
        classes you write yourself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="rendermarkdown">
      <h2>The renderMarkdown seam</h2>
      <p>
        The components that render model output — message, reasoning, context
        — do not bundle a markdown renderer. Each takes a
        <code>renderMarkdown</code> prop and defaults to plain text. The
        reference renderer, <code>react-native-enriched-markdown</code>, is a
        Fabric native module absent from Expo 57's bundled list; keeping it
        behind the prop is what leaves the core runnable in Expo Go, with
        markdown an opt-in step for dev-client apps.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="theming__onward" aria-label="Onward">
      <a href="/">Back to the home page</a>
      <a href="/components">Browse components</a>
    </nav>
  </main>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .theming {
    max-width: 64rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }
  .theming p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  /* Seam-section anchors stop clear of the sticky Header (SectionHeader idiom). */
  #textclasscontext,
  #rendermarkdown {
    scroll-margin-block-start: calc(var(--layout-header-h) + 1rem);
  }
  .theming__onward {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-block-start: 2rem;
  }
</style>
